pecific to each comparison setting varied as described in Section 4 and D . This choice reflects our goal of providing a controlled comparison: by evaluating the reuse variants against a strongly tuned PPO baseline, any observed improvement can be attributed to the data reuse mechanism itself rather than to a suboptimal baseline. Evaluation Protocol. Each method is evaluated over 10 independent runs using the same set of fixed random seeds across all compared methods. Specifically, we employed seeds in ⟦ 0 , 9 ⟧ \llbracket 0,9rbracket . Policy performance is assessed every 10000 10000 environment steps by running 50 complete episodes under the current deterministic policy, and the evaluation metric is the mean episodic return, computed as the undiscounted sum of rewards over a complete episode. For plotting purposes, the resulting mean return is smoothed via a moving average with a window of 10 evaluation points, and all plots report the mean and 95% confidence interval across runs. Final performance values reported in tables are computed as the mean over the last 10 evaluation points of each run, prior to smoothing. Diagnostics. To monitor the distributional shift induced by data reuse, we track two complementary diagnostics at every iteration. Each diagnostic is computed independently for every window index i ∈ ⟦ 0 , ω k − 1 ⟧ i\in\llbracket 0,\omega_{k}-1rbracket over the corresponding rollout set 𝒟 k − i \mathcal{D}_{k-i} , and is evaluated before the optimization phase to distinguish the shift present at the start of each iteration. Specifically, the two diagnostics are: • Clip Fraction : CF i □ = 1 D ∑ j = 1 D [ w i , j □ ∉ [ 1 − ϵ , 1 + ϵ ] ] , \mathrm{CF}^{\Box}_{i}= rac{1}{D}\sum_{j=1}^{D}\mathds{1}\!\left[w^{\Box}_{i,j}
